From: microvm
Subject: [PATCH] x86/hyperv: keep the TSC when CPUID reports it invariant

Windows Hypervisor Platform (WHP) partitions can't expose the Hyper-V
"TSC invariant controls" enlightenment, so Linux marks the TSC unstable and
falls back to the jiffies clocksource: 10 ms time resolution at HZ=100.

The VMM does report an invariant TSC in CPUID 0x80000007 EDX[8], which sets
X86_FEATURE_NONSTOP_TSC. Trust that and keep the TSC clocksource.

--- a/arch/x86/kernel/cpu/mshyperv.c
+++ b/arch/x86/kernel/cpu/mshyperv.c
@@ -666,7 +666,8 @@
 	 * need to check for it.
 	 */
 	if (!hv_root_partition() &&
-	    !(ms_hyperv.features & HV_ACCESS_TSC_INVARIANT))
+	    !(ms_hyperv.features & HV_ACCESS_TSC_INVARIANT) &&
+	    !boot_cpu_has(X86_FEATURE_NONSTOP_TSC))
 		mark_tsc_unstable("running on Hyper-V");

 	hardlockup_detector_disable();
